1. What is an object type in TypeScript?

An object type defines the expected structure of an object, including its property names and types.

const user: {
    name: string;
    age: number;
} = {
    name: "Bhargav",
    age: 27
};

2. What is a type alias?

A type alias allows us to give a reusable name to a type using the type keyword.

type User = {
    name: string;
    age: number;
};

const user: User = {
    name: "Bhargav",
    age: 27
};

3. What is an interface in TypeScript?

An interface defines a contract describing the structure an object must satisfy.

interface User {
    name: string;
    age: number;
}

Any object declared as User must contain the required properties with the correct types.

5. What are optional properties?

Optional properties are properties that an object is not required to contain. We declare them using ?.

interface User {
    name: string;
    age?: number;
}

const user: User = {
    name: "Bhargav"
};

When accessing user.age, its type is number | undefined.

6. What is the readonly modifier?

The readonly modifier prevents a property from being reassigned through TypeScript's type system.

interface User {
    readonly id: number;
    name: string;
}

const user: User = {
    id: 101,
    name: "Bhargav"
};

user.name = "John"; // Valid
user.id = 102;      // Error

It does not automatically make an object immutable at runtime.

7. How do we extend an interface?

We use the extends keyword to create an interface that inherits the properties of another interface.

interface User {
    name: string;
}

interface Admin extends User {
    permissions: string[];
}

Admin now requires both name and permissions.

8. What is declaration merging?

Declaration merging occurs when TypeScript combines multiple compatible interface declarations with the same name in the same scope.

interface User {
    name: string;
}

interface User {
    age: number;
}

The resulting User interface contains both name and age.

Unlike interfaces, type aliases cannot be redeclared with the same name in the same scope.
